text:
tags (especially XML-style) are a widely recommended best practice in modern prompt engineering, and prompts are commonly stored as plain Markdown (.md) files (or similar text files) for version control, readability, and maintainability.

XML-style tags for unambiguous, explicit boundaries and nesting. Closing tags make it clear where a section ends





&lt;role&gt;
expert at...
&lt;/role&gt;

&lt;context&gt;
    - The context provides the necessary background information for the prompt.
&lt;/context&gt;

&lt;instructions&gt;
1. Do this
2. Do that
3. Do the other thing
&lt;/instructions&gt;

&lt;rules&gt;
- Always follow the instructions.
- Never generate text that violates the rules.
&lt;/rules&gt;

&lt;examples&gt;
    &lt;example&gt;
        - This is an example of a good prompt.
    &lt;/example&gt;

    &lt;example&gt;
        - Nesting tags is possible, e.g., for a list of examples:
    &lt;/example&gt;
&lt;/examples&gt;

&lt;output_format&gt;
- markdown table

&lt;/output_format&gt;

